<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  com.claude.teammate-reap-alarm — row 4. Every 10 min: are assignee panes actually LEAVING?

  WHY A JOB AT ALL, AND WHY THIS IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THE COMMIT. The alarm already existed and
  already worked. Its only caller was bin/cc-blockers:161 — a PULL-rendered board that runs when an
  agent happens to invoke /ship — and the label appeared in no launchctl list at all. So for ten
  days the close path produced zero closes, twelve assignee panes sat resident, and the instrument
  built to say so was never asked. A sensor with no cadence is not a sensor; it is a function
  somebody could call. cc-blockers keeps its pull row unchanged — this adds the push.

  ⚠ THE ORDER OF THE TWO COMMANDS IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT COSMETIC. The alarm runs FIRST, reading the
  residency cursor left by the PREVIOUS tick, so `departed` covers one interval of wall clock. The
  sampler then advances the cursor to now. Swap them and every tick reads a cursor written moments
  earlier by itself, `departed` is 0 by construction forever, and the job reports a permanently
  dead close path on a perfectly healthy fleet. (The alarm passes --no-state precisely so that it —
  and every /ship-time cc-blockers pull — can read the cursor without consuming it. Two writers to
  one cursor means each sees only what the other left behind.)

  The alarm's own exit is what launchd records; the sampler's is discarded, since it is being run
  here for its cursor write and not for its verdict.

  VERDICTS ARE THE EXIT CODES, and non-zero is a DESIGNED outcome, not a failure to be "fixed"
  (memory: daemon-fleet-v2). OK/NOT-EXERCISED 0 · WARN 1 · ALARM 2 · NO-DATA 3 — declared as
  ok_exits in launchd/fleet.manifest so cc-fleet never files a permanent S4 row against a healthy
  job that is correctly reporting an unhealthy fleet.

  IT NEVER REFUSES ANYTHING. Both scripts are read-only by construction: no pane close, no team
  config write, no worktree touch. The only file either one writes is the residency cursor.

  PATH prepends ~/.claude/bin AND /opt/homebrew/bin via bash -c so $HOME expands at runtime. Both
  are required and neither is optional: the join needs `it2` (~/.claude/bin) and `jq` (Homebrew),
  and a bare Homebrew name does not exist under a launchd PATH (memory:
  path-resolved-dependency-in-daemon-code). Each missing tool degrades to a NAMED source in the
  verdict token — `src_win=unreachable`, `src_mem=no-jq` — never to a silent zero.

  RunAtLoad false — a login must not fire an it2 probe. C10: the OPERATOR loads it; see
  docs/activation/pending-activation/30-teammate-reap-alarm-activate.sh.
  Kill switch after load: launchctl bootout gui/$UID/com.claude.teammate-reap-alarm.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.claude.teammate-reap-alarm</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>-c</string>
        <string>export PATH="$HOME/.claude/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin"; "$HOME/.claude/scripts/teammate-reap-alarm.sh"; rc=$?; "$HOME/.claude/scripts/assignee-pane-residency.sh" --quiet >/dev/null 2>&amp;1 || true; exit $rc</string>
    </array>

    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>600</integer>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>ProcessType</key>      <string>Background</string>
    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>    <true/>
    <key>Nice</key>             <integer>10</integer>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>  <string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/teammate-reap-alarm.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/Users/chrisren/.claude/logs/teammate-reap-alarm.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
